🔥 1. Pods Running But Application Still Failing
👉 Question:
All pods are in Running state, but the application is not working properly.
💡 What interviewer expects:
You understand that Running ≠ Healthy
✅ Answer:
•	Check logs:
•	kubectl logs <pod>
•	Verify readiness probe:
o	Pod may be running but not ready
•	Check service endpoints:
•	kubectl get endpoints
•	Validate DB/API connectivity
•	Check environment variables
👉 🔑 Key line:
“Running state only means container is up, not that application is functioning correctly.”
________________________________________
🔥 2. Traffic Not Load Balanced Across Pods
👉 Question:
You have 5 pods, but traffic is hitting only one pod.
💡 Answer:
•	Check service configuration
•	Ensure:
o	Proper selector labels
o	Endpoints include all pods
•	Check:
•	kubectl get endpoints
•	Possible issues:
o	Session affinity enabled
o	Readiness probe failing on other pods
________________________________________
🔥 3. ConfigMap Updated But App Not Reflecting Changes
👉 Question:
You updated ConfigMap, but app still uses old config.
💡 Answer:
•	ConfigMaps are not automatically reloaded
Fix:
•	Restart pods:
•	kubectl rollout restart deployment <name>
👉 Senior-level:
“Or use config reload mechanisms like sidecars or volume mounts with watchers.”
________________________________________
🔥 4. Secret Exposure Concern
👉 Question:
How do you ensure secrets are secure in Kubernetes?
💡 Answer:
•	Use Kubernetes Secrets (not plain env vars in YAML)
•	Enable:
o	Encryption at rest
o	RBAC restrictions
•	Avoid:
o	Hardcoding secrets in images
👉 Bonus:
Use external secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager)
________________________________________
